---
import PlaygroundShell from '../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Simulate it" hint="The traveling salesman problem on a fixed set of cities (Manhattan distance, whole numbers). Top: solve a small instance four ways. Bottom: how the number of tours grows. Steps are counted, never timed; the time shown is an assumed number of steps per second.">
  <ol class="how">
    <li>Set <b>Cities</b> to <b>10</b> and <b>Method</b> to "Brute force: try every order": the optimum round trip is <b>326</b>, found by checking <b>362,880</b> tours in <b>986,409</b> steps. Set <b>Method</b> to "Branch and bound": the same <b>326</b> in <b>131,060</b> steps, because a partial tour that is already too long is dropped.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Method</b> to "Nearest neighbour": always go to the closest unvisited city. It takes only <b>45</b> steps but the tour is <b>456</b>, which is <b>139%</b> of the optimum. Set <b>Method</b> to "Nearest neighbour, then 2-opt": <b>108</b> steps and <b>326</b>, <b>100%</b>. On a tiny instance that can happen; 2-opt only promises a tour it cannot shorten by reversing one stretch, not the optimum.</li>
    <li>In the growth box set <b>Cities in the larger problem</b> to <b>20</b> and <b>Steps per second</b> to <b>1,000,000,000</b>: there are <b>60,822,550,204,416,000</b> tours, <b>60,822,550</b> seconds to check them all. Set <b>Cities in the larger problem</b> to <b>25</b>: <b>310,224,200,866,619,719,680,000</b> tours, <b>9,837,144</b> years.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Cities in the larger problem</b> to <b>30</b>: dynamic programming (exact, no enumeration) needs about <b>966,367,641,600</b> steps, about 16 minutes at that speed, but <b>32,212,254,720</b> stored values, about <b>258</b> GB at 8 bytes each. Exponential time becomes a memory wall.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="pg np" data-np>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <label>Cities <select class="pg-in" data-n><option value="6">6</option><option value="7">7</option><option value="8">8</option><option value="9">9</option><option value="10" selected>10</option></select></label>
      <label>Method <select class="pg-in" data-method><option value="brute" selected>Brute force: try every order</option><option value="bnb">Branch and bound</option><option value="nn">Nearest neighbour</option><option value="twoopt">Nearest neighbour, then 2-opt</option></select></label>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-grid">
      <div class="pg-box" data-b1><h3>Tour length</h3><span class="pg-mono np-big" data-len></span><span class="pg-note" data-lenn></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" style="--c: var(--good)"><h3>Optimum</h3><span class="pg-mono np-big" data-opt></span><span class="pg-note">found by checking every order</span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b3><h3>Percent of optimum</h3><span class="pg-mono np-big" data-pct></span><span class="pg-note">100% is optimal</span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" style="--c: var(--role-1)"><h3>Steps</h3><span class="pg-mono np-big" data-steps></span><span class="pg-note" data-stepsn></span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-box" style="--c: var(--border)">
      <h3>The four methods at this number of cities</h3>
      <div class="np-bars" data-bars></div>
      <span class="pg-note">Steps, log scale.</span>
    </div>
    <p class="pg-out" data-verdict role="status"></p>
    <h3>How the number of tours grows</h3>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <label>Cities in the larger problem <select class="pg-in" data-big><option value="10">10</option><option value="15">15</option><option value="20" selected>20</option><option value="25">25</option><option value="30">30</option><option value="40">40</option></select></label>
      <label>Steps per second <select class="pg-in" data-rate><option value="1000000">1,000,000</option><option value="1000000000" selected>1,000,000,000</option></select></label>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-grid">
      <div class="pg-box" style="--c: var(--role-1)"><h3>Distinct tours</h3><span class="pg-mono np-big np-wrap" data-tours></span><span class="pg-note">(n - 1)! / 2</span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-g2><h3>Time to check them all</h3><span class="pg-mono np-big" data-ttime></span><span class="pg-note" data-tsec></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" style="--c: var(--role-1)"><h3>Dynamic programming steps</h3><span class="pg-mono np-big np-wrap" data-dp></span><span class="pg-note" data-dptime></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-g4><h3>Dynamic programming stored values</h3><span class="pg-mono np-big np-wrap" data-cells></span><span class="pg-note" data-mem></span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="pg-out" data-gverdict role="status"></p>
    <p class="pg-note">Assumptions: the 10 cities are fixed points on a 100 by 100 grid, distance is Manhattan (|dx| + |dy|), the tour starts at city 1 and a tour and its reverse count once. The gap between nearest neighbour and the optimum depends on the instance: this one is on the unfavourable side. A step is a city added to a partial tour, or a pair of edges looked at by 2-opt. Dynamic programming is Held-Karp, about n squared times 2 to the n steps and n times 2 to the n values; 8 bytes per value is an assumption. The speed is assumed, nothing is timed.</p>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<script>
  import { query, formatThousands } from '../playground';
  const CITIES = [[77, 29], [81, 47], [15, 50], [49, 61], [16, 31], [92, 18], [65, 84], [52, 18], [67, 64], [75, 2]];
  const dist = (a: number, b: number) => Math.abs(CITIES[a][0] - CITIES[b][0]) + Math.abs(CITIES[a][1] - CITIES[b][1]);

  // The same model as the code samples, ported to TypeScript.
  const tourLength = (order: number[]) => order.reduce((s, c, i) => s + dist(c, order[(i + 1) % order.length]), 0);
  function bruteForce(n: number) {
    let steps = 0, tours = 0, best = Infinity;
    const path = [0], used = new Array<boolean>(n).fill(false);
    used[0] = true;
    const extend = (length: number): void => {
      if (path.length === n) { tours++; best = Math.min(best, length + dist(path[n - 1], 0)); return; }
      for (let c = 1; c < n; c++) {
        if (used[c]) continue;
        steps++;
        used[c] = true; path.push(c);
        extend(length + dist(path[path.length - 2], c));
        path.pop(); used[c] = false;
      }
    };
    extend(0);
    return { best, steps, tours };
  }
  function branchAndBound(n: number, bound: number) {
    let steps = 0, best = bound;
    const path = [0], used = new Array<boolean>(n).fill(false);
    used[0] = true;
    const extend = (length: number): void => {
      if (length >= best) return;
      if (path.length === n) { best = Math.min(best, length + dist(path[n - 1], 0)); return; }
      for (let c = 1; c < n; c++) {
        if (used[c]) continue;
        steps++;
        used[c] = true; path.push(c);
        extend(length + dist(path[path.length - 2], c));
        path.pop(); used[c] = false;
      }
    };
    extend(0);
    return { best, steps };
  }
  function nearestNeighbour(n: number) {
    const order = [0], used = new Array<boolean>(n).fill(false);
    used[0] = true;
    let at = 0, steps = 0;
    for (let s = 1; s < n; s++) {
      let pick = -1;
      for (let c = 0; c < n; c++) {
        if (used[c]) continue;
        steps++;
        if (pick < 0 || dist(at, c) < dist(at, pick)) pick = c;
      }
      used[pick] = true; order.push(pick); at = pick;
    }
    return { order, steps };
  }
  function twoOpt(start: number[]) {
    const order = start.slice(), n = order.length;
    let steps = 0, moves = 0, improved = true;
    while (improved) {
      improved = false;
      for (let i = 1; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
          steps++;
          const a = order[i - 1], b = order[i], c = order[j], d = order[(j + 1) % n];
          if (dist(a, c) + dist(b, d) < dist(a, b) + dist(c, d)) {
            for (let l = i, r = j; l < r; l++, r--) { const t = order[l]; order[l] = order[r]; order[r] = t; }
            moves++; improved = true;
          }
        }
      }
    }
    return { order, steps, moves };
  }
  function solveAll(n: number) {
    const nn = nearestNeighbour(n), nnLen = tourLength(nn.order);
    const opt = twoOpt(nn.order), optLen = tourLength(opt.order);
    const bf = bruteForce(n), bb = branchAndBound(n, nnLen + 1);
    return { bf, bb, nn, nnLen, opt, optLen };
  }

  const big = (n: number) => BigInt(n);
  function factorial(n: number): bigint { let f = big(1); for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) f *= big(i); return f; }
  function power2(n: number): bigint { let p = big(1); for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) p *= big(2); return p; }
  const group = (v: bigint) => v.toLocaleString('en-US');
  function durationText(seconds: bigint): string {
    if (seconds < big(1)) return 'under 1 second';
    if (seconds < big(60)) return `${group(seconds)} seconds`;
    if (seconds < big(3600)) return `${group(seconds / big(60))} minutes`;
    if (seconds < big(86400)) return `${group(seconds / big(3600))} hours`;
    if (seconds < big(31536000)) return `${group(seconds / big(86400))} days`;
    if (seconds < big(3153600000)) return `${(Number(seconds) / 31536000).toFixed(1)} years`;
    const years = seconds / big(31536000);
    return `${group(years)} ${years === big(1) ? 'year' : 'years'}`;
  }

  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-np]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = query(root);
    const val = (s: string) => q<HTMLSelectElement>(s).value;
    const colour = (s: string, c: string) => q<HTMLElement>(s).style.setProperty('--c', c);
    function drawSmall() {
      const n = Number(val('[data-n]')), method = val('[data-method]');
      const r = solveAll(n);
      const opt = r.bf.best;
      const rows: [string, string, number, number][] = [
        ['Brute force', 'brute', r.bf.steps, opt],
        ['Branch and bound', 'bnb', r.bb.steps, opt],
        ['Nearest neighbour', 'nn', r.nn.steps, r.nnLen],
        ['NN, then 2-opt', 'twoopt', r.opt.steps, r.optLen],
      ];
      const chosen = rows.find((x) => x[1] === method)!;
      const len = chosen[3];
      q('[data-len]').textContent = formatThousands(len);
      q('[data-lenn]').textContent = chosen[0];
      q('[data-opt]').textContent = formatThousands(opt);
      const pct = Math.floor((len * 100) / opt);
      q('[data-pct]').textContent = `${pct}%`;
      colour('[data-b3]', pct === 100 ? 'var(--good)' : 'var(--bad)');
      colour('[data-b1]', len === opt ? 'var(--good)' : 'var(--bad)');
      const shown = chosen[2]; // for 2-opt, only the 2-opt pass: the nearest-neighbour steps are listed on their own row
      q('[data-steps]').textContent = formatThousands(shown);
      q('[data-stepsn]').textContent = method === 'brute' ? `${formatThousands(r.bf.tours)} tours checked`
        : method === 'bnb' ? 'partial tours dropped once as long as the best known'
        : method === 'nn' ? 'one scan of the unvisited cities per step'
        : `${r.opt.moves} improving reversals, after the nearest-neighbour tour`;
      const bars = q('[data-bars]');
      bars.textContent = '';
      const top = Math.log10(Math.max(10, ...rows.map((x) => x[2])));
      for (const [name, , steps, length] of rows) {
        const row = document.createElement('div'); row.className = 'np-bar';
        const label = document.createElement('span'); label.textContent = name;
        const track = document.createElement('span'); track.className = 'np-track';
        const fill = document.createElement('span'); fill.className = 'np-fill'; fill.style.width = `${Math.max(2, (Math.log10(Math.max(1, steps)) / top) * 100)}%`;
        track.appendChild(fill);
        const num = document.createElement('span'); num.className = 'pg-mono'; num.textContent = `${formatThousands(steps)} steps, length ${formatThousands(length)}`;
        row.append(label, track, num); bars.appendChild(row);
      }
      q('[data-verdict]').textContent = method === 'brute' || method === 'bnb'
        ? `Exact, and fine at ${n} cities: ${formatThousands(r.bf.steps)} steps is nothing for a computer. The cost is what grows, see below. Pruning with a good starting bound needs ${formatThousands(r.bb.steps)}.`
        : pct === 100
          ? `This heuristic found the optimum here, but nothing promised it would: it is a quick answer with no guarantee, and the same method was ${Math.floor((r.nnLen * 100) / opt)}% of optimum before the 2-opt pass.`
          : `A heuristic: ${formatThousands(shown)} steps instead of ${formatThousands(r.bf.steps)}, at the price of a tour ${pct - 100}% longer than the optimum. Whether that is acceptable depends on what a longer tour costs you.`;
    }
    function drawBig() {
      const n = Number(val('[data-big]')), rate = Number(val('[data-rate]'));
      const tours = factorial(n - 1) / big(2);
      const seconds = tours / big(rate);
      q('[data-tours]').textContent = group(tours);
      q('[data-ttime]').textContent = durationText(seconds);
      q('[data-tsec]').textContent = `${group(seconds)} seconds at ${formatThousands(rate)} tours per second`;
      colour('[data-g2]', seconds > big(86400) ? 'var(--bad)' : 'var(--good)');
      const cells = big(n) * power2(n), dp = cells * big(n);
      q('[data-dp]').textContent = group(dp);
      q('[data-dptime]').textContent = `${durationText(dp / big(rate))} at ${formatThousands(rate)} steps per second`;
      q('[data-cells]').textContent = group(cells);
      const gb = Math.round(Number(cells * big(8)) / 1e9);
      q('[data-mem]').textContent = gb < 1 ? 'under 1 GB at 8 bytes each' : `about ${formatThousands(gb)} GB at 8 bytes each`;
      colour('[data-g4]', gb > 64 ? 'var(--bad)' : 'var(--good)');
      q('[data-gverdict]').textContent = n <= 12
        ? `At ${n} cities even brute force is quick. NP-hard describes how the worst case grows, not that every instance is hard.`
        : seconds > big(86400)
          ? `Checking every tour is out of reach at ${n} cities. Dynamic programming is far better than enumeration, and still exponential in time and in memory.`
          : `Still reachable by enumeration at ${n} cities with this speed, but every extra city multiplies the work by about ${n}.`;
    }
    root.addEventListener('change', () => { drawSmall(); drawBig(); });
    drawSmall(); drawBig();
  });
</script>
<style is:global>
  .playground .how li, .playground .np .pg-note { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .playground .np-big { font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 600; }
  .playground .np-wrap { overflow-wrap: anywhere; font-size: 1rem; }
  .playground .np-bars { display: grid; gap: 6px; }
  .playground .np-bar { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 9.5rem 1fr; gap: 2px 8px; align-items: center; }
  .playground .np-bar .pg-mono { grid-column: 2; }
  .playground .np-track { display: block; height: 12px; border-radius: 6px; background: var(--surface-2); overflow: hidden; }
  .playground .np-fill { display: block; height: 100%; background: var(--accent); border-radius: 6px; }
  @media (max-width: 520px) { .playground .np-bar { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .playground .np-bar .pg-mono { grid-column: 1; } }
</style>
